const MATERIALS = {
 "ipc-lec-tamices": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The three sieves (Socrates)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_socrates_tres_tamices.jpg\" alt=\"Socrates talks with a young man (François-André Vincent, 1776)\" style=\"max-width:320px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">François-André Vincent, ‘Alcibiades Receiving Instruction from Socrates’ (1776). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>Apologue of the Greek philosopher Socrates (5th-4th centuries BC). An apologue is a short fable with a lesson.</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/socrates_tres_tamices/01_un_vecino_llega_con_una_noticia.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[A neighbour arrives with some news]</strong> One day, an acquaintance came up to Socrates, very eager to talk: ‘Listen, I have to tell you something a friend of yours has done…’. Socrates stopped him: ‘Wait. Before you tell me, have you thought of passing it through the three sieves?’ The man looked at him without understanding.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/socrates_tres_tamices/02_el_primer_tamiz_la_verdad.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The first sieve: truth]</strong> ‘The first sieve is that of truth. Are you completely sure that what you are about to tell me is true?’ ‘Well… no, I was told it.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/socrates_tres_tamices/03_el_segundo_tamiz_la_bondad.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The second sieve: goodness]</strong> ‘Then you will have passed the news through the second sieve, that of goodness. What you want to tell me, is it something good?’ The man hesitated: ‘No… rather the opposite’.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/socrates_tres_tamices/04_el_tercer_tamiz_la_utilidad.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The third sieve: usefulness]</strong> ‘Let us see the last sieve, that of usefulness. Is it of any use to me to know it?’ ‘Useful… not exactly.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/socrates_tres_tamices/05_la_conclusion_de_socrates_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[Socrates' conclusion]</strong> ‘If what you want to tell me is neither true, nor good, nor useful —said Socrates—, I would rather not know it. And I advise you to forget it too.’</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Socrates would say: ‘If something is neither true, nor good, nor useful, it is better not to repeat it’. The neighbour would say: ‘Sometimes ugly things have to be told: if a friend of yours has done something bad, you have a right to know’. Which one do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving opinions.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Match each sieve with its question: truth · goodness · usefulness ↔︎ is it something good? · is it of use to know it? · is it true?</li>\n<li>In your own words: what did the man want to tell Socrates?</li>\n<li>What are the three sieves (filters) and what question does each one ask?</li>\n<li>How does the man react to each of Socrates' questions (‘no… I was told it’, ‘no… rather the opposite’, ‘useful… not exactly’)? What does that tell you about what he really knew?</li>\n<li>Socrates does not say ‘that is wrong’: he asks questions. Do you think that works better than telling him directly? Yes / No / In part, because…</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own with each term (it can be from your own life, not just from the story).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sieve</strong>: a strainer that lets the fine through and holds back the coarse; in the story, a question that filters what deserves to be said.</li>\n<li><strong>Rumour</strong>: news that goes from mouth to mouth without anyone having checked it.</li>\n<li><strong>Gossip</strong>: comment about another person's affairs, almost always negative.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: some reasons sound good and aren't; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Socrates does not let the man tell him the news and advises him to forget it too. What is he really teaching him: a) that Socrates doesn't care about his friends, b) that before repeating something you should think whether it is worth it, c) that you should never talk about anyone? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The man could defend himself: ‘I was told it by someone who doesn't usually lie. I can't check everything myself.’ Is that a good reason to believe it? And to repeat it? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ A girl finds out that someone is saying nasty things about a friend of hers behind her back. Should she tell her friend, even if it isn't ‘good’? What would her friend gain by knowing it and what would she lose? And you, what would you prefer? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n<li>If a message reaches your phone that says ‘forward it to ten contacts’, what would you ask yourself before forwarding it? Complete: ‘Before forwarding it, I would ask myself… because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: passing things through the three sieves</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before 5.</em></p>\n<p>Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of messages or news going around (at home, in the neighbourhood, on TV, on your phone), pass them through the three sieves and decide whether you would pass them on.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The message</th>\n<th>Is it true? Is it good? Is it useful?</th>\n<th>Would you pass it on? Yes / No / Depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>‘Tomorrow there's no school because of a breakdown’, without knowing who is saying it.</em></td>\n<td><em>True? I don't know. Good? Neither good nor bad. Useful? Only if it's true.</em></td>\n<td><em>Depends: before, no; after checking it on the school website, yes, because it's useful.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Which sieve seems most important to you: truth, goodness or usefulness? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>If something bad about a friend is true, should it be told to others?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Yes: if it is true, others have a right to know so as to protect themselves, as the neighbour says.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: No: if it is neither good nor useful, telling it only does harm; Socrates would rather not know.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thinks the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I'm not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · on the other hand · although · for example · I'm not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and correcting it is work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: François-André Vincent, ‘Alcibiades Receiving Instruction from Socrates’ (1776), Musée Fabre, Montpellier, public domain (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-serpiente": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The snake and the villagers",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_serpiente_y_aldeanos.jpg\" alt=\"A calm man before a cobra, while people watch (drawing from India, c. 1800-1825)\" style=\"max-width:320px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">‘Snake charmer’, drawing from Kota, India (1800-1825). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A tale from India.</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/01_la_serpiente_temida.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The feared snake]</strong> Near a village in India lived an enormous snake whose bite killed anyone who passed by. Fed up with living in terror, a group of villagers went to ask a sage for help.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/02_el_sabio_la_hace_reflexionar.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The sage makes her think]</strong> The sage talked with the snake for a long time and reproached her for her cruelty: what had the villagers done to deserve her deadly bites? He got her to reflect and promise never to attack again. And she kept her word.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/03_la_serpiente_cambia_demasiado.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The snake changes… too much]</strong> From that day she stopped being fearsome. The terrible reptile now looked like a limp, weak worm, unable even to frighten a slug.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/04_los_aldeanos_abusan.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The villagers take advantage]</strong> The villagers, who had already forgotten what she was like before, mocked her weakness: ‘What do you want fangs with venom for if you never use them?’ The children threw stones at her and kicked her.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/05_la_serpiente_vuelve_al_sabio.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The snake goes back to the sage]</strong> Tired of being beaten, the snake dragged herself once more to the sage's house: ‘I have done everything you asked, but now nobody respects me, they despise me and my heart hurts. What do you think of this?’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/serpiente_y_aldeanos/06_el_consejo_del_sabio.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The sage's advice]</strong> ‘It is very simple —the sage replied—: I asked you not to bite the villagers without reason. But did I forbid you to hiss and threaten with your tongue?’</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story recalls what the Buddha (the sage of India who founded Buddhism) called ‘the middle way’: seeking the right point between two extremes, neither biting everyone who passes nor letting yourself be walked over. Do you agree? Is there always a middle point?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving opinions.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Circle what the sage asked the snake: <strong>to leave / not to bite without reason / never to hiss</strong>.</li>\n<li>In your own words: why did the villagers go to see the sage?</li>\n<li>How did the snake change after talking to the sage? And how did the villagers treat her then?</li>\n<li>Explain in your own words the difference between ‘biting without reason’ and ‘hissing and threatening’. Which of the two did the sage ask her to give up?</li>\n<li>The villagers used to live in terror. Why do you think that, when the snake changes, they dare to mistreat her? What does that say about them?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own with each term (it can be from your own life, not just from the story).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Middle way</strong>: a Buddhist idea: seeking the right point between two extremes, neither going too far nor falling short.</li>\n<li><strong>To hiss</strong>: to make the snake's ‘ssss’ sound; in the story, warning that you will defend yourself without attacking.</li>\n<li><strong>Respect</strong>: treating someone as they deserve; it is not the same as fear.</li>\n<li><strong>Firmness</strong>: defending what you think without attacking anyone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: some reasons sound good and aren't; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The sage says to the snake: ‘But did I forbid you to hiss and threaten with your tongue?’ What is he really advising her: a) to bite again, b) to defend herself without doing harm, c) to leave the village? Explain why.</li>\n<li>A villager could say: ‘A snake is a snake. If we don't show her who's boss, any day now she'll bite again.’ Is that a good reason to treat her like that? Where is he right and where not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ On one person's swimming team there is an older boy who mocks them at every training session, and that person never defends themselves. What could they do: keep quiet, hit back, ‘hiss’ without doing harm or tell the coach? What would they gain and what would they lose? And you, in their place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n<li>The snake returns to the village the next day. Write the ‘hiss’ she could use with the children who throw stones at her: a firm sentence, without doing harm.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: hissing without biting</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before 5.</em></p>\n<p>The snake does not have to choose between biting and being walked over: she can make herself respected without doing harm. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of situations in which someone lets themselves be walked over or responds with violence (at work, in the street, in a series, in the news), and think how they could ‘hiss’ instead.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The situation</th>\n<th>How could they ‘hiss’ (defend themselves without doing harm)?</th>\n<th>Would it work? Yes / No / Depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A boss always gives the same worker the tasks nobody wants.</em></td>\n<td><em>Telling her calmly and firmly: ‘This isn't always my job; let's share it out.’</em></td>\n<td><em>Depends: yes, if the boss listens; if not, she may have to ask her colleagues for support.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Which seems worse to you: biting too much or letting yourself be walked over? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>To make yourself respected, do others need to fear us a little?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Yes: if nobody fears you, they take advantage of you; the snake is only respected again when she can frighten.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: No: respect is earned through fair treatment and clear limits, not through fear; frightening others is not respect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thinks the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I'm not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · on the other hand · although · for example · I'm not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and correcting it is work.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: ‘Snake charmer’, anonymous drawing from Kota, Rajasthan, India (1800-1825), Los Angeles County Museum of Art (LACMA), public domain (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-infierno": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "Hell and paradise (Hakuin)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_infierno_y_paraiso.jpg\" alt=\"A warrior raises his sword before an old man who does not flinch (Yoshitoshi)\" style=\"max-width:280px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Tsukioka Yoshitoshi, ‘Musashi and Bokuden’ (19th century). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A tale of the Zen master Hakuin (18th century).</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/infierno_y_paraiso/01_la_pregunta_del_samurai_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The samurai's question]</strong> A samurai came before the Zen master Hakuin and asked him: ‘Do hell and paradise really exist? And if they exist, where are their gates?’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/infierno_y_paraiso/02_hakuin_lo_provoca_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[Hakuin provokes him]</strong> Hakuin stared at him and answered with contempt: ‘And who are you to ask such a thing? You look like a beggar.’ The samurai, proud, replied that he was the best of all warriors. ‘You, a samurai? —the master went on mocking—. You don't even look like one…’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/infierno_y_paraiso/03_las_puertas_del_infierno_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The gates of hell]</strong> Red with anger, the warrior drew his sword to strike him. At that very moment, Hakuin said calmly: ‘Here the gates of hell open.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/infierno_y_paraiso/04_las_puertas_del_paraiso_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The gates of paradise]</strong> Struck by the master's serenity, the samurai understood, sheathed his sword and bowed with respect. ‘And here —Hakuin then said— the gates of paradise open.’</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that, for Zen, hell and paradise are not places, but states we create for ourselves: anger opens the gates of hell for us; self-mastery, those of paradise. Do you agree?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving opinions.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Match: ‘Here the gates of hell open’ → ☐ when the samurai draws his sword · ☐ when he bows with respect.</li>\n<li>In your own words: what did the samurai ask Hakuin?</li>\n<li>What did Hakuin do to provoke him, and how did the samurai react?</li>\n<li>Quote two sentences with which Hakuin provokes the samurai. What is he trying to hurt in him with each one?</li>\n<li>What changes in the samurai between ‘the gates of hell’ and ‘the gates of paradise’? What does Hakuin do, that the warrior does not, to make that change happen?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own with each term (it can be from your own life, not just from the story).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Samurai</strong>: a Japanese warrior of former times, with a very strict code of honour.</li>\n<li><strong>Zen</strong>: a form of Buddhism that seeks calm and attention to the present moment.</li>\n<li><strong>Anger</strong>: very strong rage that makes us lose control.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-control</strong>: the ability to hold back what we feel before acting, in order to choose how to respond.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: some reasons sound good and aren't; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Hakuin insults the samurai on purpose. Why does he really do it: a) because he dislikes the samurai, b) so that the samurai discovers in himself, at that moment, where ‘hell’ and ‘paradise’ are, c) because he wants to fight him? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The samurai could defend himself: ‘My honour is my life. If I let anyone insult me without responding, nobody will respect me.’ Is that a good reason to respond to the insult? Where is he right and where not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Someone is in the cinema queue and a boy they don't know laughs at their clothes in front of his friends. What could they do: respond angrily, walk away or answer calmly? What would they gain and lose with each option? And you, in their place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n<li>Hakuin gets the samurai to change without fighting. Invent another way in which the master could have taught him the same thing without insulting him. Would it have worked just as well? Yes / No / In part, because…</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the gate of paradise</h2>\n<p><em>Purpose: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before 5.</em></p>\n<p>Hakuin says that anger opens ‘the gates of hell’. Always? Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of situations that make people very angry, and think about what can be done with that anger.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The situation</th>\n<th>What can be done with the anger?</th>\n<th>Is it a good idea? Yes / No / Depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>At a residents' meeting, one man accuses a neighbour of something she hasn't done.</em></td>\n<td><em>She answers him right then, in front of everybody.</em></td>\n<td><em>Depends: yes if she does it calmly and with evidence; no if they end up shouting.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> A question the story leaves me with and that I can't answer: … <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>If someone is insulted, do they always have to respond?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Yes: if you don’t answer back, others walk all over you and you lose their respect; you have to make it clear that you won’t put up with it.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: No: answering with rage means losing control; you can reply calmly, walk away or ignore it, and that is strength too.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thought the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Tsukioka Yoshitoshi, ‘Musashi and Bokuden’ (19th c.), Japanese print, public domain (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-monjes": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Two Monks and the Young Woman",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_dos_monjes_y_muchacha.jpg\" alt=\"A porter crosses the Ōi river carrying a young woman on his back (Kunisada, 1853)\" style=\"max-width:420px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Utagawa Kunisada, ‘Crossing the Ōi River’ (1853). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A Zen tale (Zen is a form of Buddhism that arose in China and Japan and seeks inner calm).</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/dos_monjes_y_muchacha/01_el_rio_y_la_muchacha.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The river and the young woman]</strong> Two Zen monks were about to wade across a river when they saw a young woman approaching who also wanted to cross, but the strength of the current frightened her.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/dos_monjes_y_muchacha/02_un_monje_la_ayuda.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[One monk helps her]</strong> Without hesitating, and smiling, one of the monks lifted her onto his shoulders and carried her to the other bank.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/dos_monjes_y_muchacha/03_el_enfado_del_otro.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The other one’s anger]</strong> His companion was outraged, because the rule forbids monks to touch a woman’s body. For hours he walked along angry and sulking. Close to the monastery, he told him in an irate tone that he was going to report him to the master: ‘What you have done is shameful and our rule forbids it!’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/dos_monjes_y_muchacha/04_la_respuesta.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The reply]</strong> ‘And what is so shameful about it?’ the first asked, puzzled. ‘Helping the young woman to cross? I left her on the bank more than two hours ago… and you are still carrying her on your shoulders!’</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> The first monk would say: ‘What happened, happened: brooding over anger is to go on carrying it. And helping someone can matter more than a rule.’ The second monk would say: ‘A rule is worthless if everyone breaks it whenever they see fit.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what problem did the young woman have, and what did the first monk do?</li>\n<li>Why did the second monk get angry? How long did his anger last?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2a.</strong> What did the first monk do after crossing the river, and what did the second one do all along the way? Compare them in one sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>2b.</strong> What do you think the second monk felt as he walked (anger, envy, worry about the rule…)? Justify your answer with something from the story.</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Resentment</em>: anger that is kept inside and held on to for a long time, even after whatever caused it is over.</li>\n<li><em>Rule</em>: a norm that tells us what to do or not to do and that serves a purpose (protecting, organising, living together).</li>\n<li><em>Exception</em>: a special case in which a rule is not applied in the usual way; it has to be well justified.</li>\n<li><em>Letting go</em>: ceasing to brood over something that has already happened, so as not to go on carrying it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The first monk says: ‘I left her on the bank more than two hours ago… and you are still carrying her on your shoulders!’ What does he really mean: a) that the second monk also carried the young woman, b) that the second monk is still brooding over what happened and cannot let it go, c) that the young woman was very heavy? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The second monk could defend himself: ‘If today we break the rule for a good cause, tomorrow everyone will find their own good cause to break it. Besides, there were other ways to help her: giving her a hand, finding a stick…’ Is it a good reason? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Are there angry feelings that are worth ‘leaving on the bank’? And some that are not, because you have to talk about them or sort them out? Give an example of each (real or invented) and explain why.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>I think that… because…</em></p>\n<p><strong>5a.</strong> ✍ When something makes you angry, what helps you ‘let it go’: talking, moving about, sleeping, writing…? Begin like this: ‘I think what works best for me is… because…’.</p>\n<h2>Activity: the rule or helping?</h2>\n<p>Sometimes, in order to help someone, people break a rule. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> and think about whether it is really justified.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The rule</th>\n<th>What is done to help?</th>\n<th>Is it justified? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>No double parking.</em></td>\n<td><em>Stopping for a moment to help an older person get their bags up the steps.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if it is only a moment and it bothers no one; no if it blocks an ambulance.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> If you were the master of the monastery and the two monks told you what had happened, which one would you side with? Why?</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it better to always follow the rule, or to break it when doing so helps someone?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Always follow it: a rule that everyone breaks whenever they like stops being useful, and there is almost always another way to help without breaking it.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Break it to help: if there is a clear need and nobody is harmed, helping matters more than following the rule to the letter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Rules</em>: one person speaks at a time; every contribution comes with a reason (‘I think this because…’); we listen without interrupting and may change position if someone convinces us.</p>\n<p>My position and my best reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p><strong>Today we have thought about…</strong> (the two monks, rules and resentment)</p>\n<p><strong>A reason that convinced me / that did not convince me…</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How did I feel when speaking or listening?</strong></p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Utagawa Kunisada, ‘Crossing the Ōi River’ (1853), central panel of a triptych, Jordan Schnitzer Museum of Art, public domain (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-puercoespines": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Porcupines",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_puercoespines.jpg\" alt=\"A family of porcupines, all together (plate from 1893-1896)\" style=\"max-width:420px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">‘Crested Porcupine’, The Royal Natural History (1893-1896). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A fable by the German philosopher Arthur Schopenhauer (1788-1860).</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The cold]</strong> One cold winter’s day, a group of porcupines huddled close together to keep warm.</p>\n<p><strong>[The pricks]</strong> But the closer they pressed, the more they stuck their quills into one another, and they had to move apart.</p>\n<p><strong>[The dance]</strong> Apart, they felt cold again, and their instinct drew them together once more… and once more the pricks. They repeated this dance of coming closer and moving away many times.</p>\n<p><strong>[The right distance]</strong> Until at last they found the <strong>right distance</strong>: not so close that they hurt one another, not so far that they felt cold.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> A porcupine who felt the cold would say: ‘Better a few pricks than being cold: being with others matters most.’ One with lots of quills would say: ‘Better a bit of cold than getting pricked: everyone needs their own space.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>T or F: the porcupines huddled together to keep warm. <strong>T / F</strong></li>\n<li>In your own words: why did the porcupines huddle together, and why did they have to move apart?</li>\n<li>What did they find in the end, after coming closer and moving away many times?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The quills and the cold are not just about animals. What do they stand for among people? a) the quills are the annoyances of being too much on top of each other; the cold is loneliness. b) the quills are bad people; the cold is boredom. c) the quills are fights; the cold is winter. Explain why.</li>\n<li>Someone might say: ‘With true friends you don’t need so much distance: if you trust someone, you can tell them everything.’ Is it a good reason? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ A girl’s best friend wants to be with her at every break, writes to her at all hours and gets angry if she meets up with other people. What could that girl do: carry on as before, move away completely or look for a ‘right distance’? What would she gain and what would she lose? And you, in her place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the right distance</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>Among people, too, there is a right distance: not so close that we are a nuisance, not so far that we end up alone. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of situations in which someone gets too close or too distant, and think about what the right distance would be.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The situation</th>\n<th>What would the right distance be?</th>\n<th>Does it convince you? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A teacher asks her students about their life outside school.</em></td>\n<td><em>Showing interest if someone is unwell, but without asking in front of everyone.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if the student wants to talk; no if they prefer to keep their business to themselves.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Are you more of a porcupine who feels the cold (better close) or a porcupine with lots of quills (better with space)? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: ‘Crested Porcupine’, plate from The Royal Natural History (1893-1896), via Biodiversity Heritage Library, public domain (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-zar": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Tsar and the Happy Man’s Shirt",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_zar_camisa.jpg\" alt=\"Russian cottage (izba)\" style=\"max-width:340px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Aleksey Savrasov, ‘Izba’ (Russian cottage). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A tale from Central Europe.</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The sick tsar]</strong> In old Russia, the tsar fell ill with melancholy: nothing interested him and life seemed meaningless to him. No doctor in the empire knew how to cure him.</p>\n<p><strong>[The wise man’s remedy]</strong> Until a wise man found the solution: they had to find a <strong>completely happy</strong> man, take off his shirt and put it on the tsar; then he would be cured.</p>\n<p><strong>[The search]</strong> The emissaries travelled all over the kingdom, but it was impossible: the rich man lacked health, the healthy man lacked money, another complained about his family… Everyone had something to reproach life with.</p>\n<p><strong>[The happy man]</strong> One day, beside a wretched hut, they heard someone singing: ‘Ah, how happy I am! I have worked well, I have eaten well, now I rest! What more could I ask of life?’</p>\n<p><strong>[The surprise]</strong> They ran to buy his shirt… but the happy man was so poor that he had no shirt!</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that happiness does not lie in having many things, but in being content with what is necessary. Do you agree? Does it hold equally for everyone?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what was wrong with the tsar, and what remedy did the wise man propose?</li>\n<li>Why could they not find anyone completely happy? What surprise did they get in the end?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The only happy man in the kingdom had no shirt. What does the story mean by this: a) that to be happy you have to be poor, b) that happiness cannot be bought or passed from one person to another like a garment, c) that the wise man got the remedy wrong? Explain why.</li>\n<li>One of the emissaries might say: ‘That man is happy because he knows nothing else. With a warm house and a secure supply of food, he would be even happier.’ Is it a good reason? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Imagine that, when you grow up, you can choose between a job you love, with just enough pay to live on, and another you do not like at all, but with lots of money. Which would you choose? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the shirt that cannot be bought</h2>\n<p>Many adverts promise that we will be happy if we buy something: a car, a trip, a house, a lottery ticket, a mobile phone… Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of things that promise happiness and think about whether they can deliver it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What are they trying to sell us?</th>\n<th>What happiness do they promise us?</th>\n<th>Can it deliver? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A holiday trip.</em></td>\n<td><em>‘Switch off and be happy.’</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes, it can give a few happy days if you can afford it; no, if it leaves you with debts and worries afterwards.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, being happy is…’</p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Aleksey Savrasov, ‘Izba’ (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-mariposa": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Butterfly Dream",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_sueno_mariposa.jpg\" alt=\"Zhuangzi dreaming of a butterfly\" style=\"max-width:420px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">‘Zhuangzi dreaming of a butterfly’, traditional Chinese painting. Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>According to Zhuangzi (Chuang-tzu), a Chinese sage of Taoism, an ancient philosophy that seeks to live in harmony with nature (4th-3rd centuries BCE).</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The dream]</strong> Once there was a man who slept and dreamt that he was a butterfly. He flew from flower to flower, light and free, with the grace and fragility of a butterfly.</p>\n<p><strong>[The awakening]</strong> But suddenly he woke up and realised, to his great surprise, that he was a man.</p>\n<p><strong>[The doubt]</strong> Then he asked himself: ‘Am I a man who has just dreamt he was a butterfly? Or am I a butterfly who is now dreaming that he is a man?’</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Zhuangzi would say: ‘While we dream, everything seems real: we cannot be completely sure of anything.’ Another would say: ‘I know I am awake because everything fits together, and in dreams it does not: there is something we can know.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did the man dream, and how did he feel in the dream?</li>\n<li>What did he ask himself on waking?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The story is not just about butterflies. What does it really mean: a) that people can turn into butterflies, b) that what seems real to us sometimes is not, and it is wise not to take everything for granted, c) that sleeping a lot is bad? Explain why.</li>\n<li>While dreaming, the man was sure he was a butterfly. But someone might say: ‘If I can’t trust what I see and hear, I can’t trust anything. There must be something I can take for granted.’ Is it a good reason? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Is there anything you are completely sure of? And anything you thought was certain that turned out not to be? Give an example of each and explain why.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: real or illusion?</h2>\n<p>Even when awake we can confuse what seems real with what is real. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of things that seem real and perhaps are not, think about how to check, and decide whether you believe it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What seems real</th>\n<th>How can I check?</th>\n<th>Do you believe it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The burger in an advert: huge, juicy, perfect.</em></td>\n<td><em>Buy it and compare it with the photo.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: that they sell burgers, yes; that it is that big and good-looking, no, because advert photos are staged.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, something is real when…’.</p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: traditional Chinese painting of the butterfly dream (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-gyges": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Ring of Gyges",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_anillo_gyges.jpg\" alt=\"Gyges finds the ring inside a bronze horse (16th-century engraving)\" style=\"max-width:300px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Gyges finds the ring in a bronze horse (engraving, 1514-1531). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A Greek legend told by Plato (4th century BCE) in <em>The Republic</em>.</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/anillo_de_gyges/01_el_anillo.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The ring]</strong> Gyges was a shepherd who found a mysterious ring on the finger of a dead man. One day, while playing with it, he discovered that when he turned it <strong>he became invisible</strong>: others spoke as if he were not there.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/anillo_de_gyges/02_el_experimento.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The experiment]</strong> He tried it again and again—invisible when he turned it, visible when he turned it back—until he was convinced of the ring’s magic power.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/anillo_de_gyges/03_la_tentacion.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The temptation]</strong> But soon wicked ideas occurred to him: he began to covet the king’s riches.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/anillo_de_gyges/04_el_crimen.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The crime]</strong> Taking advantage of being invisible, he entered the palace, seduced the queen, killed the king and seized the throne.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/anillo_de_gyges/05_dos_moralejas.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> In Plato’s book, this story is told by Glaucon, and he would say: ‘With a ring like that, anyone would do the same: we do no harm <strong>only out of fear of punishment</strong>.’ Socrates would answer: ‘No: whoever does harm comes off worse even if nobody sees, because he damages himself.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did Gyges discover about the ring, and how did he make sure it was true?</li>\n<li>What did Gyges do when he knew that nobody could see him?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2a.</strong> What would have happened to Gyges if everyone had been able to see him enter the palace? What changes when he is invisible?</p>\n<p><strong>2b.</strong> The ring is magic, but the question in the story is not. What is the person telling this story really asking us?</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Anonymity</em>: a situation in which nobody knows who you are or what you are doing (like Gyges invisible, or someone with a fake profile on the internet).</li>\n<li><em>Impunity</em>: going unpunished after doing something wrong because nobody finds out or nobody can act.</li>\n<li><em>Integrity</em>: being a person all of a piece, who acts just as well when watched as when not watched.</li>\n<li><em>Conscience</em>: that inner voice that tells us whether something is right or wrong, even when there are no rules and nobody is there.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>What does the ring represent: a) a dangerous magic object, b) any situation in which we are sure nobody will see us or punish us, c) the power of kings? Explain why.</li>\n<li>Glaucon might insist: ‘Look at the roads: where there is a speed camera, everyone drives slowly; where there isn’t, many speed. People behave well out of fear of the fine.’ Is it a good reason to think we are good only out of fear? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Imagine you have the ring of Gyges for a day and you are sure nobody will find you out. Would you use it? What for: for something good, for something you don’t dare do now, or for nothing? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>I think that… because…</em></p>\n<p><strong>5a.</strong> ✍ Think of someone you admire. Do you think they would behave just as well with the ring on? What makes you think so? Begin like this: ‘I think that… because…’.</p>\n<h2>Activity: today’s rings of Gyges</h2>\n<p>Today, too, there are ‘rings’ that make it so nobody knows who you are. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of situations in which nobody sees who is doing something, and think about whether people use it for good or for ill.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Today’s ‘ring’ (nobody sees who it is)</th>\n<th>What do people do with it?</th>\n<th>Is it good that nobody sees? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The secret ballot in an election.</em></td>\n<td><em>People vote what they really think, without fear of what the boss or the family might say.</em></td>\n<td><em>Yes, because it protects the voter; although this way nobody has to explain their vote.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Whose side are you on: Glaucon’s (we do no harm out of fear of punishment) or Socrates’ (doing harm hurts us even if nobody sees it)? Why?</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Would we be good people if we were sure that nobody would ever know?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Yes: most of us would go on behaving well, because being a good person is part of who you are, not just a fear of punishment.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: No: with no eyes watching us and no possible punishment, many people would take advantage; rules work mostly through fear.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Rules</em>: one person speaks at a time; every contribution comes with a reason (‘I think this because…’); we listen without interrupting and may change position if someone convinces us.</p>\n<p>My position and my best reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p><strong>Today we have thought about…</strong> (the ring of Gyges and anonymity)</p>\n<p><strong>A reason that convinced me / that did not convince me…</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How did I feel when speaking or listening?</strong></p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: ‘Gyges finds the ring in a bronze horse’, engraving after a design by Hans Weiditz (1514-1531), Rijksmuseum, public domain CC0 (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-antigona": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "Antigone",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_antigona.jpg\" alt=\"Antigone before the body of Polynices\" style=\"max-width:460px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Nikiforos Lytras, ‘Antigone before Polynices’ (1865). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>Taken from the tragedy by the Greek poet Sophocles (5th century BCE).</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/antigona/01_la_prohibicion.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The ban]</strong> After a civil war in Thebes, King Creon ordered that Polynices, a warrior killed for having fought against his own city, should not be given burial.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/antigona/02_la_desobediencia.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The disobedience]</strong> But Antigone, the warrior’s sister, defied the ban and was arrested while burying the body.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/antigona/03_ante_el_rey.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[Before the king]</strong> Creon asked her whether she knew the law and that she risked death. ‘I knew it’, Antigone replied.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/antigona/04_la_conciencia.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[Conscience]</strong> ‘But that is only a law of men’, she said. ‘There are more important laws, those that rule deep in our hearts. My love commanded me to bury my brother. I would rather die than betray that.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/antigona/05_dos_moralejas.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Antigone would say: ‘If a law is unjust, you must follow your conscience, even if it costs you dearly.’ Creon would say: ‘If everyone obeys only the laws they happen to like, nobody can live in peace.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>T or F: Antigone knew she could die for burying her brother. <strong>T / F</strong></li>\n<li>In your own words: what did King Creon forbid, and why?</li>\n<li>What did Antigone do? Did she know she was risking death?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2a.</strong> What arguments did Creon use to defend his ban, and what arguments did Antigone use to defend her decision? Write one for each.</p>\n<p><strong>2b.</strong> Antigone knew she could die and did it anyway. What does that tell us about how important burying her brother was to her?</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Conscience</em>: the inner voice that tells us what we think is just or unjust, even when a rule says otherwise.</li>\n<li><em>Disobedience</em>: not following a rule or an order; it can be done on a whim or for a reason of justice (the latter is sometimes called <em>civil disobedience</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Unjust law</em>: a rule that, although made by those in power, seems to cause harm that nobody deserves.</li>\n<li><em>Duty</em>: what we feel we have to do, even if we don’t feel like it or it costs us dearly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Antigone says there are ‘more important laws, those that rule deep in our hearts’. What is she referring to: a) the written laws of another city, b) what our conscience tells us is just, c) what we feel like doing at any given moment? Explain why.</li>\n<li>Creon might defend himself: ‘Polynices attacked his own city. If I let everyone decide which laws to obey, tomorrow nobody will obey any and war will return.’ Is it a good reason? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ At a boy’s school they bring in a rule that he thinks is unfair: anyone who is late on one day misses the trip. What would you advise him: to simply follow it, to break it on the quiet, or to try to change it (talk to his form tutor, collect signatures…)? What would he gain and lose with each option? And you, in his place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>I think that… because…</em></p>\n<p><strong>5a.</strong> ✍ Do you think that disobeying an unjust rule is the same as breaking a rule because you don’t feel like following it? Begin like this: ‘I think that… because…’. <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Activity: fair or unfair rule?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>Without rules we cannot live together, but some may seem unfair. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of rules or laws that someone disagrees with, and think about what they could do.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The rule or law</th>\n<th>What could someone who disagrees do?</th>\n<th>Would it be justified? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Some cities ban giving food in the street to homeless people.</em></td>\n<td><em>Carry on handing out food, even if they are fined.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if those people are going hungry and there is no other help; less so if the council offers a better canteen.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> If you were Creon’s adviser, what would you tell him: to pardon Antigone or to enforce the law? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>Must we always obey the rules, or is it sometimes right to disobey them?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: We must always obey them: if each person chooses which laws to obey, living together breaks down; the right thing is to change them through legal channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Sometimes we must disobey: if a rule is clearly unjust, simply obeying it makes us accomplices in the injustice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thought the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Nikiforos Lytras, ‘Antigone before Polynices’ (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-rana": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Frog and the Scorpion",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_rana_escorpion.jpg\" alt=\"A scorpion on the back of another animal crossing the water\" style=\"max-width:460px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Fable illustration (scorpion crossing the water). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>An African tale.</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The request]</strong> On the edge of a swamp, a scorpion wanted to get to the other side. ‘Please, take me on your back and help me across’, he asked a frog.</p>\n<p><strong>[The mistrust]</strong> ‘Go on, you’re mad!’ replied the frog. ‘If I take you on my back, you’ll sting me and I’ll die.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The argument]</strong> ‘Don’t be silly!’ answered the scorpion. ‘What interest would I have in stinging you? If I sting you, you sink, and if you sink I drown too, because I can’t swim.’ By dint of insisting, he convinced her, and the frog set off on the crossing, carrying him.</p>\n<p><strong>[The sting]</strong> But in the middle of the river, the frog felt the pain of a prick and the venom paralysing her. ‘You see! You’ve stung me and we’re both going to die!’</p>\n<p><strong>[Nature]</strong> ‘I know’, replied the scorpion, ‘and I’m terribly sorry… but nobody can act against their nature.’ And the waters swallowed them both.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Someone would say: ‘Everyone is how they are: there are things about our character that we cannot change.’ Another would say: ‘We all have impulses, but we can learn to hold them back.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Circle: the scorpion stung the frog <strong>before climbing on / in the middle of the river / on reaching the bank</strong>.</li>\n<li>In your own words: what did the scorpion ask the frog, and why was she wary?</li>\n<li>What happened in the middle of the river? How did the scorpion explain what he had done?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The scorpion says: ‘I’m sorry… but nobody can act against their nature.’ If a person said that after doing harm, what would it be: a) a true reason, because they could not help it; b) an excuse for not taking responsibility; c) an apology that fixes everything? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The frog let herself be convinced because the scorpion gave her an argument that seemed logical: ‘If I sting you, I drown too.’ Was it a good reason to trust him? Where was he right and where was he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ In the message group of his football team, someone insults a boy in front of everyone and he feels a strong urge to reply with another insult. What could he do: reply straight away, wait until he is calm, or not reply? What would he gain and what would he lose? And you, in his place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: am I just like that, or can I choose?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>Sometimes we say ‘that’s just how I am’. Is that always true? Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of an impulse (from a neighbour, a footballer, a TV-series character, anyone) and of something that helps to hold it back.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The impulse</th>\n<th>What helps to hold it back?</th>\n<th>Can it be held back? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A driver who gets honked at in a traffic jam and feels like shouting.</em></td>\n<td><em>Putting on music, thinking that he doesn’t know the other person at all, waiting for the jam to move.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: if he is rested, yes; if he has had a terrible day, it is much harder.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> A question the story leaves me with and that I don’t know how to answer: … <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: fable illustration (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-diogenes": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "Diogenes and the Lentils",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_diogenes.jpg\" alt=\"Diogenes in his barrel (painting by Gérôme)\" style=\"max-width:460px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Jean-Léon Gérôme, ‘Diogenes’ (1860). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>An anecdote about Diogenes the Cynic (413-327 BCE), told by Diogenes Laertius.</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/diogenes_y_las_lentejas/01_el_filosofo_del_tonel_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The philosopher in the barrel]</strong> Diogenes lived—so it is said—inside a barrel; he loved freedom and the simple life, without luxuries, above all else.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/diogenes_y_las_lentejas/02_las_lentejas_y_aristipo_canva.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The lentils and Aristippus]</strong> One day, while he was eating a dish of lentils for supper, he was visited by Aristippus, a philosopher who lived in luxury as the king’s courtier. ‘You see?’ he said with contempt. ‘If you learned to humble yourself before the king, you wouldn’t have to make do with these wretched lentils.’ Diogenes replied: ‘And if you had learned to make do with a dish of lentils, you wouldn’t have to humble yourself before the king!’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/diogenes_y_las_lentejas/03_alejandro_y_el_sol.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[Alexander and the sun]</strong> Another time he was visited by Alexander the Great, the king who conquered an enormous empire, who admired him, and found him sunning himself. ‘Ask me for whatever you want and I will get it for you!’ Diogenes only replied: ‘Stand out of my sunlight.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/diogenes_y_las_lentejas/04_el_esclavo_manes.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The slave Manes]</strong> It is also told that his slave Manes ran away, but Diogenes did not worry or go out to look for him: ‘Manes can live without Diogenes, and Diogenes could not live without Manes? It is Diogenes who has been set free!’</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Diogenes would say: ‘Everything we have ends up having us: to be free, you must want little.’ Aristippus would say: ‘There is nothing wrong with enjoying good things; what would be wrong is not being able to choose.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Circle where Diogenes lived: <strong>in a palace / in a barrel / on a ship</strong>.</li>\n<li>In your own words: how did Diogenes live, and what did he value above all?</li>\n<li>What did Alexander the Great offer Diogenes, and what did Diogenes answer?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2a.</strong> What did Diogenes say to Aristippus about the lentils and the king? Explain it in your own words, without copying the sentence.</p>\n<p><strong>2b.</strong> Alexander the Great could give Diogenes almost anything and Diogenes wanted nothing. Why do you think he did not accept?</p>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Freedom</em>: being able to decide for yourself without depending on someone else giving or taking things away from us.</li>\n<li><em>Dependence</em>: needing something or someone so much that we cannot do what we want without them.</li>\n<li><em>Consumerism</em>: buying and accumulating things non-stop, believing that this will make us happier.</li>\n<li><em>To humble oneself</em>: to lower oneself before someone in order to get something from them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Aristippus says: ‘If you learned to humble yourself before the king, you wouldn’t have to make do with these wretched lentils.’ Diogenes turns the sentence around. What does his reply really mean: a) that the lentils taste better than the king’s food, b) that Aristippus also pays a price for his luxuries, c) that Diogenes is envious of Aristippus? Explain why.</li>\n<li>Aristippus might defend himself: ‘I eat well, I live in a palace and I talk to the king: I have many more opportunities than you, stuck in a barrel.’ Is it a good reason? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Is there anything you have (or would like to have) that makes you freer? And anything that takes your freedom away? Give an example of each and explain why. <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>I think that… because…</em></p>\n<p><strong>5a.</strong> ✍ Someone wants to buy a pair of trainers for €150. Write two questions they could ask themselves before paying. Would you ask them yourself? Yes / No / It depends, because… <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Activity: do the things we have have us?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>Diogenes said that everything we possess in turn possesses us. Always? Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of things that people have (a neighbour, a footballer, a TV-series character, anyone) and think about whether they give them freedom or take it away.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The thing</th>\n<th>Does it give freedom or take it away? How?</th>\n<th>Is it worth it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A car.</em></td>\n<td><em>It gives you the freedom to go wherever you like, but you have to pay for petrol, insurance and repairs.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if you live far from everything; less so if there is good public transport.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> If you had to live for a week as one of the two, Diogenes or Aristippus, which would you choose? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>Are you happier with few things or with many?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: With few: the less you need, the less you depend on others and the less you worry about losing what you have.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: With many (or with enough): having good things brings comfort and opportunities; what matters is being able to choose, not giving up everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thought the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Jean-Léon Gérôme, ‘Diogenes’ (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-midas": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "King Midas’s Ears",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_midas.jpg\" alt=\"King Midas, already with donkey’s ears, between Apollo and Pan (Antonio Tempesta, 1606)\" style=\"max-width:340px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Antonio Tempesta, ‘Midas receives donkey’s ears’ (1606). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>From the Latin poet Ovid (1st century BCE), <em>Metamorphoses</em>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The insult to Apollo]</strong> King Midas, who knew nothing about music, had the misfortune to declare that the satyr Marsyas (a creature of the forest, half man and half goat) played better than Apollo, the god of the arts.</p>\n<p><strong>[The punishment]</strong> Apollo, furious, pulled his ears until they turned into donkey’s ears. ‘That way, in future, you will have a keener ear’, he told him.</p>\n<p><strong>[The secret]</strong> Ashamed, Midas hid them under a large tiara. Only his barber knew the secret, and Midas warned him: ‘If you tell, you’re a dead man!’</p>\n<p><strong>[Unable to keep quiet]</strong> But the barber could hardly bear not telling it. On the fourth day he could take no more: he ran to the river, dug a hole and whispered into it: ‘King Midas has donkey’s ears!’ And he covered it up, relieved.</p>\n<p><strong>[The truth escapes]</strong> But a bed of reeds grew on the bank and, when the wind stirred it, the reeds murmured: ‘King Midas has donkey’s ears…’ And the secret took flight.</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that no secret can be hidden for ever: sooner or later, the truth comes to light. Do you agree?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Circle whom the barber told the secret to: <strong>his wife / a hole in the ground / the king</strong>.</li>\n<li>In your own words: why did Apollo punish Midas, and what was the punishment?</li>\n<li>What did the barber do to tell the secret without anyone hearing? How did it become known in the end?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>When he gives Midas donkey’s ears, Apollo tells him: ‘That way you will have a keener ear.’ Does he mean it? What does he really mean: a) that Midas will now hear better, b) that he is mocking him because he knows nothing about music, c) that he is giving him a present? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The barber might defend himself: ‘Keeping quiet was hurting me. I needed to let it out, and I found a way of doing so without harming the king.’ Is it a good reason? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ A girl has told a friend a secret that she is embarrassed about, and he finds it very hard to keep quiet. What could he do: tell it only to someone ‘trustworthy’, write it in a private chat, or keep it to himself? What would he gain and what would he lose? And you, in his place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: keep it or tell it?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>There are secrets that are best kept and others that must be told (for example, if someone is in danger). Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of secrets and decide what you would do with each.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The secret</th>\n<th>Do I keep it or tell it (and to whom)?</th>\n<th>Does it seem right to you? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>My uncle tells me he is going to leave his job, but he hasn’t told his boss yet.</em></td>\n<td><em>I keep it until he tells it himself.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes, because it is his news; but if he keeps quiet for too long, it could harm his colleagues.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Whom do you understand better: the king, who wanted to hide his shame, or the barber, who could not bear carrying the secret? Why? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Antonio Tempesta, ‘Midas receives donkey’s ears’, plate from Ovid’s Metamorphoses (1606), Rijksmuseum, public domain CC0 (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-estomago": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Stomach",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_estomago.jpg\" alt=\"Two barn owls at night, one with its prey in its talons (Audubon, 1833)\" style=\"max-width:380px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Robert Havell after J. J. Audubon, ‘Barn Owl’ (1833). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>From an Inuit tale.</em></p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/el_estomago/01_la_luna_presume.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The moon boasts]</strong> One cold winter night, the moon was boasting in the sky: ‘Look at me, I am the biggest and most beautiful, even more than the Sun’, she told the stars.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/el_estomago/02_el_lago_replica.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The lake replies]</strong> A little lake heard her: ‘That is not true! Look at yourself: I can hold all of you.’ And the moon leaned over and reflected herself in its waters.</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/el_estomago/03_la_ratita.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The little mouse]</strong> The quarrel woke a little mouse, who opened one eye, looked at the moon and the lake and exclaimed: ‘Bigger than all is my eye, because it holds the lake, the moon and all the stars at once.’</p>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/el_estomago/04_el_buho.jpg\" alt=\"\" style=\"max-width:560px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"></figure>\n<p><strong>[The owl]</strong> But a night bird that was prowling about saw her, swooped down on her and ate her: ‘I am the only one who knows who is the biggest: my stomach, which holds the mouse, her eye, the lake, the moon and all the stars in the sky.’</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> It looks like a story about pride, but it hides something more: at the base of life lies need—‘eat and be eaten’—and however much we boast, nobody is as great as they think. Do you agree?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did the moon boast about, and what did the lake reply?</li>\n<li>Why did the little mouse say her eye was the biggest? What happened to her afterwards?</li>\n<li>Who boasts of being ‘the biggest’ at each point in the story (moon, lake, little mouse, owl), and what reason do they give?</li>\n<li>What do the four boasts have in common, and what changes from one to the next? Why do you think the story ends with the owl?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own with each term (it can be from your own life, not just from the story).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To boast</strong>: to show off something of one’s own with pride so that others will admire it, sometimes exaggerating.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflection</strong>: the image of something seen on a surface, like the moon in the water of the lake; it looks the same, but it is not.</li>\n<li><strong>Food chain</strong>: the order of ‘who eats whom’ in nature; in the story, ‘eat and be eaten’.</li>\n<li><strong>Humility</strong>: recognising our worth without thinking ourselves better than others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The little mouse boasts of being the biggest… and a moment later she is eaten. What does the story mean by this ending: a) that owls are the cleverest animals, b) that, however much we boast, nobody is as great as they think: we all need to eat and may end up being eaten, c) that mice should not go out at night? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The owl says: ‘My stomach is the biggest, because it holds the mouse, her eye, the lake, the moon and all the stars.’ Is it a good reason for saying it is the biggest? Is there anything in it? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Is boasting always wrong? Think of one time when you thought it was fine for someone to boast about something (it can be you) and another when it bothered you. What is the difference?</li>\n<li>Complete: ‘Something I am proud of without needing to boast about it is… and I know this because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: who is ‘the biggest’?</h2>\n<p>Today, too, there are those who boast of being ‘the biggest’ or ‘the best’. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of people, brands or countries that do so, and look at what reason they give.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who is boasting, and about what?</th>\n<th>What reason do they give?</th>\n<th>Is it a good reason? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A bar: ‘The best omelette in town!’</em></td>\n<td><em>That it won the town fiesta competition.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if the jury tasted many omelettes; less so if only three entered.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence and explain why: ‘For me, being “the biggest” is…’.</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it better to boast about what you have achieved or to stay humble?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Boasting about what you have really achieved is fair: you have the right to feel proud and to let it show.</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Humility is better: whoever boasts is annoying and, like the moon or the little mouse, may end up looking ridiculous.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rule for turns</strong>: you have to defend the position your teacher assigns you (not the one you would choose). Each turn lasts one minute, begins with ‘I think that… because…’ and must give at least <strong>one reason</strong>. We listen without interrupting and, after each turn, the other position answers that reason (not the person). At the end, everyone says whether they have changed their mind and which argument moved them.</p>\n<p>My main reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p><strong>Today I have learnt that…</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What made me think most was…</strong></p>\n<p><strong>One thing I am going to try or change from now on is…</strong></p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Robert Havell after John James Audubon, ‘Barn Owl’ (1833), National Gallery of Art, public domain CC0 (via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-narciso": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "Narcissus",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_narciso.jpg\" alt=\"Narcissus gazing at himself in the water (Caravaggio)\" style=\"max-width:420px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Caravaggio, ‘Narcissus’ (c. 1597-99). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>From the Latin poet Ovid (1st century BCE), <em>Metamorphoses</em>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The beautiful indifferent one]</strong> Narcissus was so handsome that all the girls sighed for him. But, brought up as a spoilt child, none of them interested him, and he broke all the hearts around him.</p>\n<p><strong>[The curse]</strong> Spurned, one of them begged the gods: ‘May he who loves no one fall in love with himself!’ And the gods granted it.</p>\n<p><strong>[The reflection]</strong> One morning, beside a spring, Narcissus bent down to drink and in the water discovered a face so beautiful that he fell in love at once… without understanding that it was his own reflection.</p>\n<p><strong>[The despair]</strong> He spoke to it and got no reply; when he leaned in to kiss it, the water clouded and the face disappeared. Believing himself rejected, he went mad and plunged a dagger into his chest: ‘Farewell, my love!’</p>\n<p><strong>[The flower]</strong> The gods took pity and, on the spot where his blood fell, made a flower grow which they called narcissus, like him.</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that loving oneself is necessary—you cannot love others without loving yourself a little—but that loving yourself too much can leave us blind to others. Do you agree? Where would you draw the line?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>T or F: Narcissus knew the face in the water was his own. <strong>T / F</strong></li>\n<li>In your own words: what was Narcissus like, and how did he treat the girls?</li>\n<li>What did one of the girls ask of the gods? What then happened to Narcissus beside the spring?</li>\n<li>Why do you think the girl asked for precisely that punishment? What is the connection between what Narcissus did and what happens to him afterwards?</li>\n<li>Narcissus falls in love ‘without understanding that it was his own reflection’. What does he not know? Would it have changed anything if he had known?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spurned</strong>: suffering and hurt because the person one loves does not love one back.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflection</strong>: the image of something or someone seen on a surface, such as water or a mirror.</li>\n<li><strong>Vanity</strong>: thinking oneself better than others and needing everyone to admire us.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-esteem</strong>: valuing and loving oneself in a healthy way, neither despising oneself nor thinking oneself superior.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own (not from the story) using one of these terms.</p>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>Narcissus’s punishment is to fall in love with his own reflection. What does the story mean by that: a) that it is dangerous to look at yourself in the water, b) that whoever loves only himself ends up alone and unable to see others, c) that the gods are cruel? Explain why.</li>\n<li>Someone might say: ‘Looking after your image and liking yourself is nothing bad. If you don’t value yourself, nobody will.’ Is it a good reason? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"7\">\n<li>✍ When do you think it is right to think of yourself first? And when is it not? Give an example of each and explain why. <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n<li>A girl is an invented influencer: she posts twenty photos a day and checks the ‘likes’ every five minutes. Her brother says she loves herself too much; she says it is her job and that she looks after herself. Who is right? Yes / No / In part, because…</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: loving yourself well or loving yourself too much</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>Loving ourselves is necessary; the problem comes when we look only at ourselves. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of behaviours and decide whether they are loving yourself well or loving yourself too much.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The behaviour</th>\n<th>Loving yourself well or too much?</th>\n<th>Does it seem right to you? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A sportswoman says in an interview: ‘I am the best in the world.’</em></td>\n<td><em>It could be loving yourself well (she trusts herself) or too much (she looks down on her rivals).</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if she says it to encourage herself; no if she says it to humiliate the others.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, loving yourself well is…’. <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<h2>Two positions (in writing)</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it better to think mainly of yourself or to think of others first?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Think of yourself first (if you don’t look after yourself, nobody will).</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Think of others first (whoever looks only at himself ends up alone, like Narcissus).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose A or B and copy the reason that convinces you most. Add one of your own.</p>\n<p>Someone who thought the opposite would say: ___</p>\n<p>I would answer: ___</p>\n<p>☐ You may read my answer without my name</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: Caravaggio, ‘Narcissus’ (public domain, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-damocles": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Sword of Damocles",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_damocles.jpg\" alt=\"Damocles, on the throne, looks in terror at the sword hanging by a hair above his head\" style=\"max-width:360px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>A story preserved by the Latin poet Horace (1st century BCE).</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The envy]</strong> Dionysius the Elder, tyrant (a ruler with absolute power) of the city of Syracuse, in Sicily, gave great banquets where he displayed his wealth and power. One of his courtiers, Damocles, dazzled by so much luxury, dared to tell him how much he envied him.</p>\n<p><strong>[The exchange]</strong> ‘Since all this seems so enviable to you’, the tyrant replied, ‘would you like to try it and find out for yourself what my lot consists of?’ Damocles, delighted, accepted.</p>\n<p><strong>[The luxury]</strong> They sat him in a golden chair adorned with precious stones, perfumed him and served him the most exquisite delicacies. Damocles floated on a cloud of happiness…</p>\n<p><strong>[The sword]</strong> …until he raised his eyes and discovered, hanging above his head, a sharp steel sword held by nothing more than a horsehair. In that instant he stopped seeing the luxury, tasting the food, hearing the music.</p>\n<p><strong>[The escape]</strong> ‘Have you seen what my lot is? Have you had enough?’ ‘More than enough!’ whispered Damocles, and he got up at once from that not-at-all-enviable chair. Because whoever lives always in fear cannot enjoy the slightest pleasure.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> Damocles, after the fright, would say: ‘No wealth makes up for living in fear: better a quiet life.’ Dionysius would say: ‘Power is frightening, yes, but it also lets you do great things; somebody has to sit under the sword.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did Damocles envy, and what did Dionysius propose to him?</li>\n<li>What did Damocles discover above his head? What happened to him then?</li>\n<li>What did Damocles feel before seeing the sword and what did he feel afterwards? The chair, the food and the music did not change: so what was it that changed?</li>\n<li>Dionysius asks him: ‘Have you seen what my lot is?’ What do you think he means by ‘lot’? Do you think Dionysius was happy? Give reasons.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tyrant</strong>: a ruler who governs with absolute power, with no limits and nobody to check him.</li>\n<li><strong>Courtier</strong>: a person who lives at court, beside the king, and seeks his favour.</li>\n<li><strong>Envy</strong>: sadness or unease at seeing that someone else has something we do not have.</li>\n<li><strong>Horsehair</strong>: the long, strong hair from a horse’s mane or tail; here, the thread that holds the sword.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own (not from the story) using one of these terms.</p>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>Dionysius did not really want to give Damocles his place: he wanted to teach him something. What does the sword hanging by a single hair represent: a) that there were dangerous weapons in the palace, b) the threats and fear that always go with power, even though from the outside only the luxury is seen, c) that Damocles was a bad guest? Explain why.</li>\n<li>Damocles might insist: ‘All right, there is a sword. But Dionysius still has gold, banquets and all the power: many people would accept the fear in exchange for that.’ Is it a good reason for wanting his life? Where is it right and where is it not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"7\">\n<li>✍ Imagine you are offered the chance to be team captain or class representative: more fame, but also more pressure, more criticism and more fear of failing. Would you accept or would you prefer a quieter life? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n<li>Think of something you envy in another person (their fame, their money, their grades, their looks). What ‘sword’ might there be that you cannot see? Begin like this: ‘I envy… but perhaps I don’t see that…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the sword you cannot see</h2>\n<p>From the outside, some lives and some positions look perfect. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of lives or positions that provoke envy (in politics, sport, a company, your neighbourhood…), think about what their ‘sword’ might be and decide whether it is worth it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Which life or position provokes envy?</th>\n<th>What might its ‘sword’ be?</th>\n<th>Is it worth it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Prime Minister.</em></td>\n<td><em>Bodyguards, criticism every day and fear of getting it wrong in decisions that affect millions of people.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if you want to change things and can stand the pressure; no if you are only after fame.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> If Dionysius offered you his golden chair for a year, sword included, would you accept? Why?</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it worth having great power or fame even if it brings fear and pressure?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Yes, it is worth it: power lets you do great things and somebody has to take the risk (Dionysius).</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: No, it is not worth it: no wealth makes up for living in fear; a quiet life is better (Damocles).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rules for turns</strong>: positions are allocated (if necessary, at random: you may have to defend the one you were not thinking of). Each person speaks for one 30-second turn and <strong>always with a reason</strong> (‘I think that… because…’); to answer someone, first sum up what they have said (‘You say that…, but…’). Nobody speaks again until everyone has spoken. At the end, anyone who wishes may change position and explain which reason convinced them.</p>\n<p>My position and my best reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p>‘Today I have learnt that…’</p>\n<p>‘What made me think most was… because…’</p>\n<p>‘Something I envy and now look at differently is…’</p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-lobo": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Wolf and the Dog",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. Adapted from <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_lobo_perro.jpg\" alt=\"The wolf looks at the mark of the collar on the dog’s neck; in the background, the collar and chain\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><em>By the French poet Jean de La Fontaine (1621-1695).</em></p>\n<p><strong>[The meeting]</strong> A hungry wolf was wandering through the forest when he came across a huge, handsome dog. By instinct he would have attacked him, but the dog was big, so he treated him with humility and even paid him compliments on his fine appearance.</p>\n<p><strong>[The offer]</strong> ‘It depends only on you to look as well as I do’, the dog told him. ‘Leave the forest, where you are starving, and come with me.’ ‘And what will I have to do?’ ‘Very little: drive away beggars and flatter the master; in return, he will give you as much food as caresses.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The collar]</strong> The wolf, starving, could not believe his luck and set off with him. But on the way he noticed that the dog’s neck was bare of fur. ‘What have you got there?’ ‘Oh, nothing… it’s the mark of the collar I’m tied by.’</p>\n<p><strong>[Freedom]</strong> ‘Tied!’ exclaimed the wolf. ‘Do you mean you can’t run wherever you like?’ ‘Not always, but what does it matter?’ ‘Well, I think nothing in the world matters so much’, said the wolf. ‘And at that price, you can keep your meals and even the greatest treasures.’ And he went back to the forest, where, they say, he still roams free.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> The wolf would say: ‘Nothing is worth as much as going wherever you want: better to go hungry than to live tied up.’ The dog would say: ‘Eating every day and being safe is worth a great deal too: a collar is not such a bad price.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did the dog offer the wolf, and what would he have to do in return?</li>\n<li>What did the wolf discover on the dog’s neck? What did he then decide?</li>\n<li>At the start, the wolf was hungry and would have attacked the dog. Why did he not? What does that say about how people behave towards those who are stronger?</li>\n<li>The dog says what has to be done is ‘very little’. Is it really little? Look in the story for words that contradict it.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To flatter</strong>: to pay exaggerated compliments to someone, often to get something (to butter them up).</li>\n<li><strong>To drive away</strong>: to chase someone or something out of a place by frightening them.</li>\n<li><strong>Freedom</strong>: being able to decide for yourself where to go and what to do, without being tied to another’s will.</li>\n<li><strong>Comfort</strong>: living without effort or worries, with what you need to hand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own (not from the story) using one of these terms.</p>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>The dog says the mark on his neck is ‘nothing’ and that being tied ‘doesn’t matter’. What does the collar represent in the story: a) a simple decoration, b) the freedom that is lost in exchange for comfort, c) a punishment for behaving badly? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The dog might defend himself: ‘In the forest you go hungry, cold and in danger. I eat every day and someone looks after me. What use is being free if you starve?’ Is it a good reason? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"7\">\n<li>✍ Almost all of us accept some ‘collar’ in exchange for something good: the rules at home, those of a team, a timetable… Think of one of yours. Is it worth it for you? What do you gain and what do you lose?</li>\n<li>Someone offers you something very good (a new phone, being popular, a good position) in exchange for always doing what you are told. What would you answer? Begin like this: ‘I would tell them that… because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the price of the collar</h2>\n<p>The dog lives comfortably, but he wears a collar. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of something comfortable or safe that has its ‘collar’, and think about whether it is worth it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The comfortable thing</th>\n<th>The ‘collar’ (what is lost in exchange)</th>\n<th>Is it worth it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A permanent job with good pay.</em></td>\n<td><em>You have to keep to a timetable, obey the boss, and it is not so easy to change your life.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes if it gives peace of mind and you like it; less so if it sours your days.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> If you had to live for a year as one of the two, wolf or dog, which would you choose? Why?</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Is it better to be free even if you go hungry, or to live safely even if you are tied up?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Better free: nothing is worth as much as going wherever you want (the wolf).</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Better safe: eating every day and being protected makes up for a collar (the dog).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rules for turns</strong>: positions are allocated (if necessary, at random: you may have to defend the one you were not thinking of). Each person speaks for one 30-second turn and <strong>always with a reason</strong> (‘I think that… because…’); to answer someone, first sum up what they have said (‘You say that…, but…’). Nobody speaks again until everyone has spoken. At the end, anyone who wishes may change position and explain which reason convinced them.</p>\n<p>My position and my best reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p>‘Today I have learnt that…’</p>\n<p>‘What made me think most was… because…’</p>\n<p>‘A “collar” I wear and perhaps should review is…’</p>\n<p class=\"src\">Image: AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-rey": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Limping King",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. A tale collected by the French poet Gustave Nadaud, included in <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_rey_cojeaba.jpg\" alt=\"The lion king and his courtiers (Grandville, ‘The Lion’s Court’)\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Grandville, ‘The Lion’s Court’ (La Fontaine’s Fables, 1838). Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><strong>[The fashion at the palace]</strong> Once upon a time there was a king who limped. His courtiers, to please him and win favours, began to approach him limping. Little by little the custom spread: the ministers limped, the guards limped, the cooks limped… In the palace there was nobody left who walked straight.</p>\n<p><strong>[The newcomer]</strong> One day a gentleman from outside arrived at court, whom nobody had told about the custom. He entered the throne room and walked past the king as straight as a poplar.</p>\n<p><strong>[The laughter]</strong> The whole room began to snigger: how ridiculous, a man who doesn’t limp! Everyone laughed… except the king, who went red with anger. ‘Sir! What is the meaning of this? It seemed to me that you are not limping!’</p>\n<p><strong>[The reply]</strong> The gentleman, without losing his composure, bowed: ‘Your Majesty, you are mistaken. Both my feet are covered in corns. If I walk straighter than anyone, it is because I limp on both feet at once.’</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> The gentleman would say: ‘One should not imitate what makes no sense, even if everyone does it and even if they laugh at you.’ A courtier would say: ‘With a powerful king, adapting is not cowardly but clever: even the gentleman had to find an excuse.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: why did the courtiers limp, if they were not lame?</li>\n<li>What happened when the gentleman entered the throne room? How did the court and the king react?</li>\n<li>What was the palace ‘custom’ and how did it start? Had the king ordered it?</li>\n<li>When the gentleman walked past straight, the whole room laughed instead of asking themselves why they were limping. Why do you think they laughed?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Vocabulary and concepts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Courtier</strong>: a person who lives at court, beside the king, and seeks his favour.</li>\n<li><strong>Conformity</strong>: doing what the majority does so as not to stand out, even if it makes no sense.</li>\n<li><strong>Irony</strong>: saying something with a meaning other than the literal one, often to mock without it showing.</li>\n<li><strong>‘Everybody does it’</strong>: this is not a reason by itself: that many people do something does not show it is right (it is called the appeal to the majority, or <em>ad populum</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write a sentence of your own (not from the story) using one of these terms.</p>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>The gentleman tells the king: ‘I limp on both feet at once.’ How do you think he says it: a) in earnest, because his feet really hurt; b) as a joke, mocking the custom without the king noticing; c) to butter up the king and escape punishment? Explain why. (Hint: how does someone walk who limps on both feet at once?)</li>\n<li>A courtier might defend himself: ‘I don’t limp because I like it. I limp because otherwise the king is offended and I lose my post, and I have a family to support.’ Is it a good reason? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"7\">\n<li>✍ If you were the gentleman, what would you do: start limping like everyone else, tell the truth, or find another way out? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n<li>Think of a fashion or custom in your class, your group or the social media you follow that you follow without quite knowing why. Would you follow it all the same if nobody saw you? Begin like this: ‘Everyone does…, and I…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: who is limping here?</h2>\n<p>Today, too, there are ‘kings’ whom many imitate (a boss, a politician, a company that sets the fashion, the popular ones in a group) and ‘limps’ that are copied without anyone asking why. Is imitating always bad? Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who is being imitated?</th>\n<th>What is being imitated (the ‘limp’)?</th>\n<th>Does it make sense to imitate it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The boss of an office.</em></td>\n<td><em>Everyone stays on to work late because he stays.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes, if there really is a lot of work; no, if it is only so that the boss sees them.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘A question the story leaves me with and that I don’t know how to answer: …’</p>\n<h2>Quick debate</h2>\n<p><strong>Should you follow the customs of the group to get along, or is it better to go your own way even if they laugh at you?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Position A</strong>: Follow the group: adapting avoids problems and is not cowardly but clever (the courtier).</li>\n<li><strong>Position B</strong>: Go your own way: one should not imitate what makes no sense, even if everyone does it (the gentleman).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rules for turns</strong>: positions are allocated (if necessary, at random: you may have to defend the one you were not thinking of). Each person speaks for one 30-second turn and <strong>always with a reason</strong> (‘I think that… because…’); to answer someone, first sum up what they have said (‘You say that…, but…’). Nobody speaks again until everyone has spoken. At the end, anyone who wishes may change position and explain which reason convinced them.</p>\n<p>My position and my best reason:</p>\n<h2>Wrap-up: learning journal</h2>\n<p>‘Today I have learnt that…’</p>\n<p>‘What made me think most was… because…’</p>\n<p>‘Something I do because everyone does it and that I would like to question is…’</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-palillos": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Ivory Chopsticks",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. A tale by the Chinese philosopher Han Fei, included in <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_palillos.jpg\" alt=\"The prince shows the ivory chopsticks to his worried teacher; behind them, luxuries grow until they reach a cracked wall\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><strong>[The whim]</strong> Long ago, in a kingdom of China, a young prince ate every day with simple bamboo chopsticks. One day he saw some ivory chopsticks, white and gleaming, and took a fancy to them: ‘I want some like that! They’re only chopsticks, I’m not asking for much.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The teacher is worried]</strong> His old teacher turned very serious. ‘Your Majesty, they are not just chopsticks. When you have them, you will realise they do not go with the clay bowls on our table, and you will want jade bowls. With jade bowls, the usual rice and vegetables will seem like very little, and you will want the rarest and costliest dishes.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The ladder]</strong> ‘And when you eat like an emperor’, the teacher went on, ‘you will no longer want to wear plain clothes or live in an ordinary house: you will ask for silks and palaces. To pay for it, you will raise taxes on the peasants. They will become poor and wretched, and the kingdom will sink. Your ivory chopsticks are like a tiny crack in a wall: it seems nothing is happening, but through it the whole wall ends up falling.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The decision]</strong> The prince fell to thinking. He forgot his whim and, over the years, became a king famous for his wisdom.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> The teacher would say: ‘Each desire leads to another; if you don’t learn to stop, someone ends up paying the bill.’ The prince, at the start, would say: ‘One whim does not always lead to another: you can enjoy something beautiful without wanting the whole kingdom.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did the prince want, and why did he think it did not matter?</li>\n<li>Put the teacher’s ‘ladder’ in order: what would the prince go on asking for after the chopsticks? Who would end up paying for it all?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The teacher says the chopsticks are ‘like a tiny crack in a wall’. What does he mean: a) that ivory chopsticks break easily, b) that one small whim opens the way to others that grow ever bigger, c) that the kingdom’s walls were badly built? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The prince says: ‘They’re only chopsticks, I’m not asking for much.’ Is it a good reason for buying them? Where is he right and where is he not? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Has it ever happened to you that having something new made you want more things? And the other way round: something you had and that was enough for you? Give an example of each and explain why.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: the ladder of desire</h2>\n<p>Some purchases make us want others, like the prince’s chopsticks. Always? Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of something that, once you have it, makes you want more things (you, your family, anyone).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What is bought first</th>\n<th>What is wanted next</th>\n<th>Is it worth it? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A house with a garden.</em></td>\n<td><em>A lawnmower, garden furniture, a barbecue, a swimming pool…</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes, if it is enjoyed and can be afforded; no, if it is bought only to have the same as the neighbours.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, a whim is fine when…, and stops being fine when…’</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-hachas": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Axe Thief",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. A Chinese parable attributed to the sage Liezi (Lie Yukou), included in <em>Les Philo-fables</em> by Michel Piquemal (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_hachas.jpg\" alt=\"A woodcutter with a bundle of firewood (Sessen, Japanese album leaf)\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Sessen, ‘Woman and Woodcutter’, album leaf (Japan, 18th-19th c.), Brooklyn Museum. Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><strong>[The lost axe]</strong> One morning, a peasant went to chop wood and could not find his axe anywhere. He looked in the shed, in the vegetable garden, under the cart… Nothing. Then he remembered that the neighbour’s son had gone past the day before. ‘It was him’, he thought.</p>\n<p><strong>[The suspect]</strong> From that moment he began to watch the boy. And everything seemed suspicious to him. If the boy walked fast, it was because he was hiding something. If he greeted him with a smile, it was a thief’s smile. If he kept quiet, it was because he felt guilty. Even his face, the peasant thought, was the typical face of an axe thief.</p>\n<p><strong>[The woodpile]</strong> A few days later, while gathering logs beside the woodpile, the peasant saw something gleaming among the wood: his axe! He had left it there himself and forgotten.</p>\n<p><strong>[Another look]</strong> The next day he saw the neighbour’s son again. He walked the same way, smiled the same way and had the same face as ever. But now the peasant did not think he looked like a thief at all. The boy had not changed in the slightest. What had changed was the way of looking at him.</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that, once we have made up our minds about someone, we see evidence everywhere: the problem is not in the other person but in how we look. Do you agree? Or is the first impression sometimes right?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: why did the peasant suspect the neighbour’s son?</li>\n<li>Write two things the boy did that seemed ‘like a thief’ to the peasant. How did those same things seem after he found the axe?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The story says: ‘The boy had not changed in the slightest. What had changed was the way of looking at him.’ What does it mean: a) that the boy learnt to hide it, b) that the peasant saw in the boy what he already believed about him, c) that the peasant had poor eyesight and needed glasses? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The peasant might defend himself: ‘I didn’t accuse anyone, I only suspected. The boy was here just the day before: who else was I going to suspect?’ Is it a good reason to suspect? And to accuse him? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’, and say what would be needed to have real proof.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Imagine a mobile phone goes missing in class and everyone starts looking at a classmate ‘who looks the type’. What would you do: join in the suspicions, keep quiet, or ask for proof before accusing? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: prejudice detective</h2>\n<p>Sometimes we judge someone from a single clue or from their appearance. Other times, a clue does say something. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> in which someone draws conclusions from what they see (in the street, at work, in the news, in class). There is no need to give names.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What is seen</th>\n<th>What is thought</th>\n<th>Is it a good clue? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A neighbour sees a stranger at night trying the doors of several cars.</em></td>\n<td><em>‘He is trying to steal.’</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: it is a good reason to keep an eye out or to report it, but it is not proof; perhaps he cannot find his car.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, real proof is…’</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-elefante": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Chained Elephant",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. An idea from a story by Jorge Bucay, retold in other words (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_elefante.jpg\" alt=\"A huge elephant tied to a tiny stake and a boy who does not understand it\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">AI-generated illustration (Nano Banana Pro) for educational use.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><strong>[The circus]</strong> As a small boy, he loved circuses, and above all the elephant: a gigantic animal, able to lift logs and knock down a wall if it wanted. But, between shows, the elephant stood still, tied by one leg with a chain to a small stake, barely driven into the ground.</p>\n<p><strong>[The question]</strong> The boy did not understand: with one tug, the elephant would pull up the stake and leave. Why did it not do so? He asked the grown-ups. ‘Because it’s tame’, they told him. ‘And if it’s tame, why do they tie it up?’ he insisted. Nobody gave him an answer that made sense, and over the years he forgot about the matter.</p>\n<p><strong>[The answer]</strong> Much later he discovered the explanation: that elephant had been tied to a similar stake when it was a calf. Back then the stake really was too strong for it. It pulled, pushed and wore itself out trying, day after day… until one day it stopped trying. It learnt that it <strong>could not</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>[The real chain]</strong> The adult elephant does not escape because it still believes what it learnt when small. It never again put its strength to the test. What holds it is not the stake: it is the memory of ‘I can’t’.</p>\n<p><strong>A possible moral.</strong> The story seems to say that often it is not things that tie us down but the ideas we have about ourselves, and that to find out whether you can now, you have to try again. Do you agree? Or are there times when ‘I can’t’ is true?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In your own words: what did the boy not understand when he saw the circus elephant?</li>\n<li>What happened to the elephant when it was a calf? Why does it not escape now that it is an adult?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The story says that what holds the elephant is not the stake but the memory of ‘I can’t’. So what does the stake represent: a) a very strong iron chain, b) an idea about yourself that is no longer true, c) the trainer’s punishment? Explain why.</li>\n<li>The grown-ups told the boy: ‘It doesn’t escape because it’s tame.’ Is it a good reason? Does it really explain why the elephant does not leave? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ Think of something about which you once said ‘I’m no good at this’ (drawing, public speaking, a sport…). Would you try it again now? What would you gain and what would you lose?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: today’s stakes</h2>\n<p>Sometimes what holds us back is not what is happening now but an idea we learnt a long time ago. Other times, the ‘I can’t’ is true. Find <strong>two examples from today (not from the story)</strong> of ‘I can’t’ ideas that hold people back (you, your family, anyone; there is no need to give names) and think about whether they are still true.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The ‘stake’ (what someone believes they cannot do)</th>\n<th>Where does that idea come from?</th>\n<th>Is it still true? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>An adult thinks: ‘I’m too old to learn to swim.’</em></td>\n<td><em>As a child he swallowed water in the pool and was frightened.</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: no, if it is only the fear from back then; yes, if he now has a health problem that prevents it.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Whom do you agree with more: the person who says ‘if you try, you can’ or the one who says ‘you have to know your own limits’? Why?</p>"
 },
 "ipc-lec-zorro": {
  "subject": "ipc",
  "tema": "Critical thinking · Readings to think about",
  "title": "The Fox Who Pretended to Be the Tiger",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A story to think with. Read it slowly and then answer the questions in your notebook or your journal. A traditional Chinese tale from the <em>Zhanguo ce</em> (<em>Strategies of the Warring States</em>, 1st c. BCE), the origin of the saying 狐假虎威 (‘the fox borrows the tiger’s might’) (own version).</p></blockquote>\n<figure style=\"margin:0 0 1.2rem;text-align:center\"><img src=\"media/lecturas/lec_zorro_tigre.jpg\" alt=\"A tiger crouching among reeds (Ju Lian, 1886)\" style=\"max-width:400px;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:10px\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption style=\"font-size:.85em;opacity:.7;margin-top:.35rem\">Ju Lian, ‘Reclining Tiger’ (1886), Chinese painting. Public domain.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2>The story</h2>\n<p><strong>[The trap]</strong> A hungry tiger was looking for food in the forest and caught a fox. As he was about to eat him, the fox, quite calm, said: ‘Careful! You cannot eat me. The Emperor of Heaven has appointed me king of all the animals. If you eat me, you will disobey Heaven.’</p>\n<p><strong>[The test]</strong> The tiger hesitated. ‘Don’t you believe me?’ the fox went on. ‘Walk behind me and you will see how all the animals run away the moment they see me.’ The tiger thought this a good way to check, so he agreed.</p>\n<p><strong>[The walk]</strong> The fox set off with his head held high and the tiger followed close behind. The deer, the rabbits and the wild boars, on seeing them come, ran off to hide. The tiger was impressed: ‘It’s true, they are all afraid of him.’ And he let the fox go.</p>\n<p><strong>[What the tiger did not see]</strong> The animals were not running away from the fox. They were running away from the tiger, who was walking right behind. The fox had no power at all: he had only ‘borrowed’ it from the tiger, and the tiger did not even realise.</p>\n<p><strong>Two possible morals.</strong> The tiger, if he found out, would say: ‘Before believing what you see, check why it is happening.’ The fox would say: ‘When you are weak, using your head is not cheating: it is saving your life.’ Which do you side with?</p>\n<h2>Understanding the story</h2>\n<p><em>What for: making sure we have understood the same thing before giving our opinion.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"0\">\n<li>Circle who the animals were running away from: <strong>the fox / the tiger / both</strong>.</li>\n<li>In your own words: what did the fox tell the tiger so that he would not eat him?</li>\n<li>Who were the animals really running away from? Why did the tiger not realise?</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A · Thinking rigorously: is it a good reason?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: some reasons sound good and are not; today we practise spotting them.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>The fox proposes a test: ‘Walk behind me and you will see how everyone runs away.’ Why was it a trap: a) because the animals already knew the fox, b) because the tiger, by walking behind, was exactly what frightened the animals, c) because the fox ran faster than the tiger? What should the tiger have done to settle his doubts?</li>\n<li>Sometimes we listen to someone because of who backs them: if the doctor says ‘this is recommended by the Health Service’, you trust it. Is it a good reason to believe someone because of the power or fame of another person behind them? When yes and when no? Answer: ‘Yes / No / In part, because…’.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>B · Thinking for yourself: what would I do?</h2>\n<p><em>What for: deciding for yourself, knowing what you gain and what you lose. Do 5a or 5b: you choose.</em></p>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li>✍ In someone’s team, a boy tells them: ‘If you don’t always pass me the ball, I’ll tell my cousin, who’s the leader of the older kids.’ What could that person do: obey, check whether it is true, or stand firm? What would they gain and what would they lose? And you, in their place? <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Activity: spot the fox</h2>\n<p><em>What for: seeing that this happens today, outside the story. You can do it before question 5.</em></p>\n<p>There are people, companies and adverts that lean on the power of someone else (a brand, a celebrity, a group, a position, a body…). Sometimes it is a trap and sometimes it is not. Find <strong>two ‘foxes’ from today (not from the story)</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Who is the ‘fox’?</th>\n<th>Whose power is being borrowed (the ‘tiger’)?</th>\n<th>Is it a good reason to trust them? Yes / No / It depends, because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>A dental clinic.</em></td>\n<td><em>A sign says: ‘Recommended by the Dental Association.’</em></td>\n<td><em>It depends: yes, if it is true and can be checked; no, if it is just a nice-looking stamp that nobody awarded.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Your turn.</strong> Finish the sentence: ‘For me, someone has real power when…’ <em>(Only the teacher reads this answer.)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>☐ Today I prefer not to give an opinion (it counts if the rest of the minimum is done) · ☐ You may read my answer without my name · ☐ You may read it with my name</p>\n<p>To say what you think: ‘I think that ___ because <strong><em>’ · ‘I disagree with X because </em></strong>’ · ‘X is right about <strong><em>, although </em></strong>’ · ‘I’m not sure about <strong><em>; what would change my mind is </em></strong>’. (X is a character or an author, never a classmate.)</p>\n<p>Word bank: I think that · because · whereas · although · for example · I’m not sure about.</p>\n<p>Second pass in another colour: correcting also earns marks. Making a mistake and fixing it is working.</p>\n</blockquote>"
 }
};
